On Thu, 06 Jul 2006 01:48:39 +0200, Patric Mueller wrote:

> I don't want to belittle Ray Chasons achievement. As far as I can
> judge he's done a wonderful job.
> 
> But one hard rule when doing translations is that you never translate
> *to* a foreign language. You should always translate *from* a foreign
> language *to* your native language. 

Far from belittling my work, you give me more credit than I really
deserve.  My project eventually drew attention from native speakers, and I
got corrections from about a half dozen correspondents in Spain, Mexico
and Argentina.  So much of Spanish NetHack was indeed translated into and
not out of the translators' native language.  This includes, particularly,
almost everything in dat/ other than the dungeon description files ending
in .des.

The earliest versions were my own work and they were quite bad.

In the end, my role in Spanish NetHack has been more technical than
linguistic; I did point out a few places where I thought my correspondents
had misunderstood the English, but mainly what I did was bring Spanish
NetHack out of the realm of wouldn't-it-be-nice, and keep it going forward
as the DevTeam released new versions.

